#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf9bf9f-954c-57b6-a123-694b1a50d9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨4,\\frac{3}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365097} {:answer/id #uuid "36ff2f93-fb80-5679-aeef-15ab8bfea1b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨8,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365098} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9e0fed-379c-5ade-ae18-79c30a3c6439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨4,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365099} {:answer/id #uuid "bb700b28-d74f-5af7-a1e3-21ad4b4ad474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365100} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2af7d6-e366-5356-b2f3-667207aef115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨8,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ff2f93-fb80-5679-aeef-15ab8bfea1b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨8,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365098} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e191d0f-5b18-5a36-805a-ed686c90d1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36bd380d-dae8-5b76-9cc2-25429c7a8051" :question/math-academy-id "q-122086" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3{t}^{2},t+\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,-1⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},t+\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (t+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{1}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{3},\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{2}^{3},\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{2}{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "507c7290-8ad6-5e80-9893-d6cb9be7b884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨5,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365104} {:answer/id #uuid "10a859fa-7e36-5797-a4bf-c5f6fe12264d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨3,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365105} {:answer/id #uuid "c42f42e5-4718-532a-a10e-a289685a31b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-1,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365106} {:answer/id #uuid "d551da63-3fed-5a9c-9934-1b946500fc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365107} {:answer/id #uuid "3e13ecf5-986b-561f-9f66-8b992b35b37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e13ecf5-986b-561f-9f66-8b992b35b37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365108} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d4d6ec-9913-5019-aa2b-3fe949dca787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7df19fa-04ae-5351-a8a0-d4ee8b09fbbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-122092" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,1⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1-3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (4t-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},2{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨2-{2}^{3}+{C}_{1},2({2}^{2})-2+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,1⟩ & =⟨-6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=5$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+5,2{t}^{2}-t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1-{1}^{3}+5,2({1}^{2})-1-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3688b68c-18ba-511c-9e22-058359eb4353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365111} {:answer/id #uuid "7e29080d-1ea8-58cd-b87d-35c16a0d7273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365112} {:answer/id #uuid "38b991eb-5c9e-5da0-b004-b7a9bab76248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365113} {:answer/id #uuid "9c07faeb-191d-57ab-bf76-696abe41914d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365114} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1d9568-db1f-5203-b761-f7036652a8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38b991eb-5c9e-5da0-b004-b7a9bab76248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365113} :answer-field/id #uuid "69dfe4a1-c122-520c-b52d-a5393989bc74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be1d00ae-0d99-5592-b106-53e15e2080df" :question/math-academy-id "q-122097" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t-3},2⟩,\\,t>3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2⟩$ when $t=5,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t-3},2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t-3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(5)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(5) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-3)+{C}_{1},2(5)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1},10+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-8.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3),2t-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3),2t-8⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=4, \\\\ 2t-8=2(t-4) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=4,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e28e634-7a03-5e25-8321-b7695d5e0008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1, 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085350} {:answer/id #uuid "9616670d-c7e3-52d4-b5ff-a745291cfe55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085351} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ae56e4-e460-5f2e-bf28-134c9dbe6574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085352} {:answer/id #uuid "f540a970-844d-5b89-970f-cc9c05d5d050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085353} {:answer/id #uuid "39095918-e206-5a17-b1bb-3faa1e2219af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39095918-e206-5a17-b1bb-3faa1e2219af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085354} :answer-field/id #uuid "19bef875-68ab-5dda-a3d0-5267ed95b739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1587ed41-dd82-54e2-80a7-d4c3863f391d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31632" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨4t+4,6t-21⟩\\,,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨42,\\,-18⟩$ when $t=4,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4t+4,6t-21⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (6t-21)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}+4t+{C}_{1},\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨42,\\,-18⟩.$ Substituting $t=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =⟨2(4)^{2}+4(4)+{C}_{1},\\,3(4)^{2}-21(4)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨42,\\,-18⟩ & =⟨48+{C}_{1},\\,-36+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=18.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{2}+4t-6,\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+18⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2}+4t-6,\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+18⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+4t-6=2(t-1)(t+3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-3,1, \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-21t+18=3(t-1)(t-6) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "510ea6a5-8cda-529e-802a-5c50f1d0600d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365118} {:answer/id #uuid "6d57aed9-ad9d-5edd-9b75-5e29f420772a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365119} {:answer/id #uuid "96ad4f3d-e14d-5e5d-98f0-a53bdc5f0f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365120} {:answer/id #uuid "fe196f84-50d0-5fce-bdb3-9ba5f4897f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365121} {:answer/id #uuid "af3e33f9-776e-5844-9694-a32948454c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d57aed9-ad9d-5edd-9b75-5e29f420772a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365119} :answer-field/id #uuid "09463fad-df2a-5772-9549-1abf1f98c9c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41270051-ca64-57eb-8b58-5bfbd46339fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-83928" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)⟩,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{2} & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}]}
